  {
    "episode_id": 81286,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81286.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "well, I think there's a lot we could do that. I think could be bipartisan, right? So for example, the fact that the we haven't ended the pandemic emergency makes no sense to me. And that's actually a mechanism the executive branch is using to spend way more money than traditionally they're allowed to do it through executive fiat. So that ending that is, I think, a no-brainer that, you know, a common sense, Wisconsin, I'd be a Democrat or Republican could get behind. Now that being said, if you end the pandemic emergency, you're ending Title 42. So you need to enhance border security at the same time because we have a complete absolute disaster with the Southern border right now. The other than that sensible to me, you know, really the issue driving our debt is not, you know, any of our discretionary programs that we've we authorize and appropriate every year. It's the mandatory programs. No one wants to talk about mandatory programs. So I don't think someone should come into discussion saying we do XYZ on Social Security, but there are bills out there that could create a bipartisan process for at least looking at the long-term impact of our mandatory programs. There's something called the Trust Act, which is bipartisan in the Senate. I think 70 senators voted for it in the last Congress. I have that bill in the House, so hopefully that will be part of the discussion. But I'm not coming into this saying, you must pass my bill. I just want the president to start talking with us.",
