  {
    "episode_id": 81286,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81286.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "All right, got it. I kind of want to stick with that. The Social Security and Medicare issue, because Ron Johnson has been hit quite a bit on that for arguing that they should be turned from mandatory spending into discretionary spending, is there a way to kind of save Social Security and Medicare without putting those two programs up for votes like every year?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I want to stick with the current approach.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Ron Johnson has faced criticism regarding his position on Social Security and Medicare funding.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Ron Johnson supports changing Social Security and Medicare from mandatory to discretionary spending.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "There may be ways to preserve Social Security and Medicare without making them subject to annual legislative votes.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Annual votes on entitlement programs create political instability and risk their long-term sustainability.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Preserving Social Security and Medicare requires maintaining stable, predictable funding mechanisms.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Discretionary spending status would expose Social Security and Medicare to greater political negotiation and uncertainty.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that some alternative mechanism exists that could ensure program integrity while avoiding annual appropriations.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views the current system of mandatory spending as preferable to annual voting cycles.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 81286,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81286.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "There is social security is admittedly easier than Medicare. But one thing you could do that would be sort of a generational fix. And I think anyone coming in this discussion has to be clear about we're not talking about changing anything for anybody at or near retirement at all. But basically our generation, I don't, I'm not going to hesitate to guess how old you are, but you look younger than me. So I'm saying like millennials and I'm an old millennial and below we're scheduled to get more generous benefits. than our parents will get. So in a real basis, right? If you adjust for inflation, we're going to get a more generous compensation package like that, that doesn't need to happen. Just give us the same square deal that our parents and our grandparents get and you start to fix a lot of problems. So there's all sorts of sensible things you could do like that, you know, on the Medicare side, it's more complex, healthcare is maddeningly complex, but You can have auto enrollment and Medicare Advantage, for example, and then allow people the choice to opt in or out of it. You can find efficiencies here or there. But there I see less of a silver bullet solution and more of a kind of like sensible healthcare reform efforts over time that add up that reduce costs while not compromising services that patients need as well as coverage. But that's a harder thing to do in one piece of legislation.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Social Security is easier to address than Medicare.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "No changes should be made to individuals at or near retirement age.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "A generational fix could involve adjusting future benefits for millennials and those younger.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
