  {
    "episode_id": 81286,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81286.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "I'll answer that one first, and I'll answer your previous question. I think those concerns are misplaced. I mean, if you look at my record the last six years, I don't think any Democrat could accuse me of inflaming anti-Asian hatred. My rhetoric is rarely reckless, though I often make mistakes. I have a bipartisan track record. And so listen, I guess the proof will be in the pudding, but the most obvious way where we can blunt that criticism is to constantly draw a distinction between the Chinese Communist Party and the Chinese people. And the Chinese Communist Party is the primary oppressor of the Chinese people. The Chinese diaspora is often the primary victim of CCP aggression and defending that diaspora, defending Asian Americans from any transnational repression or coercion regime is going to be one of our core functions on the Select Committee on China, which leads to your first question. I think the most urgent priority for our overall grand strategy vis-a-vis China is to make the right investments when it comes to hard power in the Indo-Pacific. We need to arm Taiwan to the teeth before it's too late. That gives us our best chance of preventing war. That's the lesson of Ukraine where we saw deterrence fail because we pulled hard power from Eastern Europe and we relied solely on soft power. Let us not repeat that same mistake. in Ukraine, that to me is the most urgent aspect of our policy and something that we could really make a difference on in the next two years. The second dimension of this competition is what I call economic statecraft. So there we need to do a better job of ensuring that US dollars are not funding genocide in China, that US dollars are not funding China's military modernization, that we are not funding our own destruction. So putting in place sensible controls on U.S. Capitol flowing outbound to China, I think is something we can get done in this Congress. And then when it comes to sort of the third dimension of competition, what I would call ideological competition or ideological warfare, we need to understand something called United Front Work. United Front Work is something Xi Jinping, the General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party, has called this magic weapon. It's kind of a combination of espionage and influence operations. It's a way to corrupt a lot of our domestic institutions, we need to understand it in order to develop policies and legislation in order to counter United Front Work. And there I would say all of our universities, including the University of Wisconsin system, need to do a better job of being on the lookout for Chinese influence. A lot of these universities are desperate for Chinese cash, and that can corrupt free speech on universities. So we want to make sure that we're not messing up what used to be like the crown jewel of Western civilization, which was, you know, the university system, you know, in the UK and then ultimately in America. And so those are some a few thoughts on what we can do.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Chinese Communist Party is the main oppressor of the Chinese people.",
